\begin{quote}
Learning Languages: Unlocking Paths to\hspace{0pt}New
Horizons\hspace{0pt}

Written by: Karen Jia\hspace{0pt}\\
Published: February 22, 2026\hspace{0pt}

At the beginning of English class, my teacher posed the following
question to me and my\hspace{0pt}eighth grade classmates: ``Where do you
think you would find the oldest,\hspace{0pt}\\
continuously-operating university in the world?'' We each wrote our
responses on a piece\hspace{0pt}of paper. Afterward, my teacher asked
everyone to share their answers.\hspace{0pt}

An overwhelming majority of students responded with cities like
``Oxford'' or ``Boston.\hspace{0pt}instinctively associating
intellectual longevity with Western institutions. To our
surprise,\hspace{0pt}my teacher revealed that the right answer was, in
fact, the University of al-Qarawiyyin,\hspace{0pt}recognized by Guinness
World Records and UNESCO as the oldest operating university
in\hspace{0pt}the world. This university is located in Fez, Morocco and
was founded in the year 859 AD\hspace{0pt}by Fatima al-Fihri during the
Islamic Golden Age. It was one of the greatest
leading\hspace{0pt}educational institutions of its time.\hspace{0pt}

My classmates' homogenous answers revealed more than a gap in historical
knowledge;\hspace{0pt}they reflected how centuries of Western
imperialism and globalization have positioned\hspace{0pt}Western
knowledge as the default. The problem was not that my classmates
were\hspace{0pt}unintelligent--- it was that many of us had been limited
to a single culture, and thus, world\hspace{0pt}view.\hspace{0pt}

Expanding our cultural knowledge can break through this mindset.
Learning new\hspace{0pt}\\
languages is not merely a tool for communication; it reshapes how we
think, broadens our\hspace{0pt}empathy, and enables individuals-- and
therefore societies-- to engage with the world in a\hspace{0pt}more
thoughtful and responsible manner.\hspace{0pt}

I grew up in a bilingual household with Chinese immigrant parents.
Although it is common\hspace{0pt}among immigrant families to prioritize
teaching English to their children to fit in, my\hspace{0pt}parents
insisted on speaking Chinese at home. Looking back, I am grateful for
every daily\hspace{0pt}conversation and every annual summer trip to
China. Maintaining Chinese preserved my\hspace{0pt}connection to my
heritage and expanded my worldview.\hspace{0pt}

Thanks to my access to another language, I consumed English and Mandarin
media,\hspace{0pt}noticing differences in culture. Over time, I realized
that certain Chinese words and\hspace{0pt}expressions even carried
nuances in meanings that could not be fully translated
into\hspace{0pt}English. Now I can acknowledge that learning languages
is truly the best way to\hspace{0pt}understand a culture
fully.\hspace{0pt}

This personal experience aligns with research. Studies by
the\hspace{0pt}\hspace{0pt}\hspace{0pt}show that multilingual people
often demonstrate\hspace{0pt}

and cognitive flexibility because they constantly manage multiple
linguistic systems.\hspace{0pt}Switching between languages strengthens
attention control and the ability to adapt to new\hspace{0pt}gitudinal
research even suggests that bilingualism may\hspace{0pt}\hspace{0pt},
reinforcing that language learning\hspace{0pt}\hspace{0pt}strengthens
the brain across a\hspace{0pt}lifetime.\hspace{0pt}
\end{quote}

