\begin{quote}
Starting elementary school, I began studying French and Japanese. With
each new set of\hspace{0pt}vocabulary, grammar, and ideas I was
introduced to, I was putting on a different set of\hspace{0pt}glasses to
interpret the world. By communicating with speakers of different
languages,\hspace{0pt}one naturally becomes aware that no perspective is
universal.\hspace{0pt}

Despite these proven facts, some argue that language learning isn't that
essential.\hspace{0pt}Particularly with artificial intelligence on the
rise, translating between languages has\hspace{0pt}become more
convenient than ever.\hspace{0pt}

Still, digital translators can't keep up with cultural fluency; they
fail to capture the different\hspace{0pt}worldviews that a different
culture brings to the table. As stated before, many ideas
and\hspace{0pt}values aren't able to be directly translated as they may
have originated independently\hspace{0pt}through a certain culture. Lack
of ability to interpret and transmit human emotions
results\hspace{0pt}in inflexible interactions.\hspace{0pt}

The benefits of learning multiple languages extend beyond the
individual. In the United\hspace{0pt}States, Western culture is often
regarded as the default. This subtle standard can
cause\hspace{0pt}limited thinking, lack of creativity, and ignorance
perhaps can even evolve into arrogance.\hspace{0pt}It is important to
note that this does not mean rejecting Western culture, ideas, and
values,\hspace{0pt}but recognizing that when a single narrative
dominates, our ability to question it weakens.\hspace{0pt}Learning new
languages can prevent this and widen the horizons of every
person.\hspace{0pt}

According to the\hspace{0pt}\hspace{0pt},
people\hspace{0pt}\hspace{0pt}who are fluent in more than
one\hspace{0pt}language contribade, diplomacy, healthcare, and
national\hspace{0pt}security. The United States also just happens to be
a nation of immigrants from all across\hspace{0pt}the world. Diverse
languages bring diverse ways of thinking, and that is what makes
our\hspace{0pt}society stronger.\hspace{0pt}

Globally, language learning promotes cooperation and mutual
respect.\hspace{0pt}s that we can learn from. Only after understanding
each other can we\hspace{0pt}cooperate and move forward as global
citizens.\hspace{0pt}

The question my English teacher asked that morning was not really about
geography. It\hspace{0pt}was about perspective. Why did so many of us
instinctively center our answers on the\hspace{0pt}West? Because
language and culture quietly shape what we assume to be the
academic\hspace{0pt}center of the world.\hspace{0pt}

Language is more than a tool for communication; it is a lens through
which we interpret\hspace{0pt}the world around us. Essentially, it's
important for us to seek out perspectives that are\hspace{0pt}different
from what we're used to. This way, we are less likely to limit ourselves
to one way\hspace{0pt}of thinking. By learning new languages, we unlock
not only new cultures, but new ways of\hspace{0pt}thinking--- and move
closer to becoming more thoughtful individuals, a stronger
nation,\hspace{0pt}and a more cooperative global community.\hspace{0pt}

